1. Encapsulation : 
Encapsulation is the process of bundling data and the methods that operate on that data into a single unit, such as a class. It also helps control access to the data 
Real-Time Example: Bank Account 🏦

In a banking application, a customer's balance should not be directly accessible or modified by anyone.

Instead, the balance is kept private, and users interact with it through methods such as deposit() and withdraw().
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.Inheritance:
Inheritance is a mechanism in which a child class inherits properties and methods from a parent class. It also promotes code reusability 
Real-Time Example 🚗

A Car is a type of Vehicle, so it can inherit common properties and behaviors such as speed, start, and stop from the Vehicle.
The Car can also have its own specific features, such as openTrunk() or playMusic().
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Abstraction:
	Abstraction is the process of hiding unnecessary implementation details and showing only the essential features to the user 

Real-Time Example: Online Payment : 💳

When you make an online payment, you simply click:
"Pay Now"

You don't need to know:
> How the payment request is encrypted
> How the bank server is contacted
> How authentication happens
> How the transaction is processed
> How the response is received

You only interact with the essential functionality.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.Polymorphism: 
Polymorphism means many forms. It allows the same method or interface to behave differently depending on the object. 

Real-Time Example: Payment System : 💰

Imagine an application supporting multiple payment methods:
> Credit Card
> UPI
> PayPal
All of them can have a pay() method, but each payment method performs the operation differently.
